Checkpoints go through the replay batcher

jbd_journal_flush_trans -- the path that flushes a committed transaction's
blocks home, at unmount for the whole checkpoint queue and mid-workload
when the ring wraps -- read each logged block back with one device command
and wrote it home with another, serialized, in log order. Eject has an OS
timeout just as mount does. The recovery machinery already solves both
sides (0027's read window, 0028's sorted/deduplicated/coalesced batch);
this routes the checkpoint through it: resident copies are recorded from
the live buffer, evicted ones read from the log through a window capped to
the transaction's own span, and the home writes land as coalesced runs.

An honest sizing note: eager release-time flushing means most transactions
are already checkpointed by the time an ordinary eject runs, so the common
case was never the audit's worst case. What this path really owns is the
uncommon ones -- blocks re-journaled by a later transaction (whose older
copies can only be checkpointed from the log), the emergency purge when
the ring fills under load, and ejects on volumes with deep queues.

Three correctness gains ride along:

* an escaped block (first word matching the journal magic) checkpointed
  from the log was written home with its escaped bytes -- the escape bit
  lived only in descriptor tags the checkpoint never read. It now travels
  on the jbd_buf, set at commit time.

* completions run after the batch lands, one per buffer with the batch's
  verdict, so 0031's tail-freeze semantics survive the rewrite (the
  bad-sector cell in Tests/run_eio_tests.sh keeps proving it).

* the transaction is lifted off the checkpoint queue for the completion
  drain, so no cascade can free the structure the loop is walking --
  the same lifetime discipline 0039 introduced.

The old body remains as the out-of-memory fallback. The eject cell in
Tests/run_replay_speed_tests.sh prices a write flood plus its unmount
under the media model and bounds the command and flush counts.

